From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 11:20:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dsi: wait for the MDP to go idle before a command
 mode DCS transfer

On a command mode panel the MDP pushes every frame through the same DSI
controller that carries DCS commands. dsi_cmds2buf_tx() guards the video
mode case -- a command has to fit inside a BLLP -- but does nothing at
all for command mode: it kicks the command DMA whenever userspace asks,
including while a frame transfer is in flight.

The two then contend. The controller reports it:

	dsi_err_worker: status=5

which is DSI_ERR_STATE_TIMEOUT | DSI_ERR_STATE_FIFO, and the panel shows
bands of corrupted lines, because it is latching pixel data that arrived
late or truncated. On a Motorola Moto G82 5G (SM6375, Novatek NT37701,
DSC 1.1 command mode) this is reproducible any time brightness is
changed while the compositor is repainting: KWin's ambient brightness
ramps down with 60-180 DCS writes spread over 3.5-7.2 s, and the bands
appear for as long as it lasts, with 70 of those errors logged in one
run. Occasionally the corruption is bad enough that the whole panel goes
a dark crimson until the next modeset.

The tell is that it cannot be reproduced by writing the backlight from
sysfs, at any rate, including the same 14 writes/s KWin uses and
including 3200-step jumps. Those land on a static screen, and a static
command mode panel has no frame transfers to contend with. It needs both.

The controller already exposes the bit for this and the header already
defines it -- DSI_STATUS0_CMD_MODE_MDP_BUSY -- it was simply never read.
Downstream polls exactly this register before touching the link, in
dsi_ctrl_hw_cmn_wait_for_cmd_mode_mdp_idle(), with a 2 ms interval and a
200 ms timeout; use the same values.

A timeout is deliberately not fatal. Falling through and sending the
command is better than dropping it, which would leave the panel holding
stale state -- a brightness that never arrives is worse than one that
arrives badly.

This affects every command mode panel on msm, not just this one.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/err.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
+#include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_graph.h>
@@ -1568,6 +1569,35 @@
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * In command mode the MDP pushes each frame through this same
+	 * controller, and starting the command DMA while one of those transfers
+	 * is in flight makes the two contend: the hardware reports
+	 * DSI_ERR_STATE_TIMEOUT | DSI_ERR_STATE_FIFO and the panel shows bands
+	 * of corrupted lines, because it is latching pixel data that arrived
+	 * late or truncated.
+	 *
+	 * The video mode case is already handled above -- commands have to fit
+	 * in a BLLP -- but the command mode case was not handled at all, even
+	 * though the controller exposes exactly the bit needed for it. So wait
+	 * for the MDP to go idle first. Downstream does the same thing with the
+	 * same register in dsi_ctrl_hw_cmn_wait_for_cmd_mode_mdp_idle(), with
+	 * the same poll interval and timeout.
+	 *
+	 * A timeout here is not fatal: fall through and send anyway rather than
+	 * drop the command, which would leave the panel with stale state.
+	 */
+	if (!(msm_host->mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO)) {
+		u32 status;
+
+		if (readl_poll_timeout(msm_host->ctrl_base + REG_DSI_STATUS0,
+				       status,
+				       !(status & DSI_STATUS0_CMD_MODE_MDP_BUSY),
+				       2 * USEC_PER_MSEC, 200 * USEC_PER_MSEC))
+			pr_warn_ratelimited("%s: timed out waiting for MDP idle\n",
+					    __func__);
+	}
+
 	ret = dsi_cmd_dma_tx(msm_host, len);
 	if (ret < 0) {
 		pr_err("%s: cmd dma tx failed, type=0x%x, data0=0x%x, len=%d, ret=%d\n",
